{% comment %}
  blocks/_product-bundle-category.liquid — private.

  One category pill in `sections/product-bundle.liquid`, and the list of products
  behind it.

  ## Why the products live in the block and not in the section

  The section could have taken one collection and one set of filters, and the
  pills would then be tags or metafield values read off that collection. That is
  fewer settings and it is the wrong shape for this section: the drawing's
  categories are Gut, Sleep, Skin + Hair, Recovery, Perform, Essentials, Focus
  and Immunity — merchandising groups a merchant curates, not facets that happen
  to exist in the data. A merchant wants to put three specific products under
  Sleep and reorder them, which a tag cannot express.

  So each pill is a block with its own collection, its own label and its own
  icon, and reordering the pills is reordering the blocks.

  ## Private, and rendered through `content_for 'blocks'`

  The leading underscore keeps it out of every other section's block picker. It
  is a repeatable block rather than a static one, because the number of
  categories is the merchant's to choose — unlike the header, which there is
  exactly one of and which `sections/product-bundle.liquid` renders through
  `content_for 'block'` for that reason.

  ## It renders a panel, not a pill

  The pill is drawn by the section, because the whole strip has to be one
  `role="tablist"` with one tab stop through it — `assets/collection-tabs.js`
  owns the roving tabindex, and a tab that renders itself cannot know its index
  in the strip. The block renders the `role="tabpanel"` and the rows inside it,
  and publishes the pill's label, icon, icon size and colours to the section
  through `block.settings`.

  That split is why the section reads `section.blocks` for the strip and then
  calls `content_for 'blocks'` for the panels: two passes over the same blocks,
  one for each half of the pattern.

  ## The label does not fall back to the collection

  It used to be `s.label | default: collection.title`, which put "Skin and hair
  care supplements" on a pill built for "Skin + Hair" — a collection title is
  written for a collection page and is the wrong length for a capsule in a rail.
  Worse, it moved: picking a different collection silently renamed the tab.

  So the pill's name is the merchant's field and nothing else, and an empty one
  falls back to a translated placeholder rather than to data. That is the rule
  `blocks/_tab.liquid` already follows, where the default is the word "Tab".

  ## The icon is the theme's icon architecture

  `icon_source` / `icon` / `icon_size` / `icon_size_custom`, resolved through
  `snippets/icon-size.liquid` and drawn by `snippets/icon.liquid` — the same
  four settings, the same names and the same option list every other block in
  the theme that carries an icon uses. It was a bespoke twelve-value select with
  no size control and no "none" that matched nothing else in the editor.

  ## The list is the theme's carousel, vertical

  `snippets/carousel-shell.liquid` with `direction: 'vertical'` — the same
  component, the same `data-options`, and the same Navigation, Pagination,
  Control bar, motion and colour settings every other carousel in the theme
  reads, turned on its side. There is no picker-specific slider and there must
  not be one.

  The controls are rendered `detached`, in the panel's own row below the track,
  because a vertical carousel clips to its own height: arrows placed outside the
  track and a row of dots below it would both be drawn where there is nothing to
  see them.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings
  assign collection = s.collection
  assign limit = s.products_limit | default: 6 | plus: 0

  comment
    `section` is a global in Shopify Liquid, so a block can read the settings of
    the section it is inside without them being threaded through — which is what
    is wanted here. How many rows are on screen, whether ratings show and how
    high a row's stepper can go are decisions about the whole list, so they are
    set once on the section rather than repeated on every category.
  endcomment
  assign show_rating = section.settings.show_rating
  assign show_quantity = section.settings.show_row_quantity
  assign max_per_product = section.settings.max_per_product | default: 4 | plus: 0

  comment
    The pill's name. No collection fallback — see the note above.
  endcomment
  assign label = s.label
  if label == blank
    assign label = 'products.bundle.category_placeholder' | t
  endif

  assign panel_id = 'bundle-panel-' | append: block.id
  assign carousel_id = 'bundle-carousel-' | append: block.id
  assign tab_id = 'bundle-tab-' | append: block.id

  comment
    ---- the tab this panel belongs to ----

    Drawn here and moved into the strip by `assets/collection-tabs.js`, because
    `block.blocks` does not exist in Liquid: the Tabs block cannot read its
    children's settings, so it cannot draw their buttons. The same constraint
    `blocks/tabs.liquid` and `assets/tabs.js` were built around, met again.

    A `<template>` rather than a label on a data attribute, because the button
    has an icon in it — an SVG escaped into an attribute is markup no formatter
    indents and no checker validates, and `snippets/icon.liquid` could not have
    produced it. `data-label` stays as the fallback and as the accessible name
    if the template holds nothing but decoration.

    The theme's icon architecture, not a select of its own: `icon_source`
    decides whether there is one at all, `icon_size` and `icon_size_custom`
    resolve through `icon-size` to the same five steps every other block offers,
    and `icon.liquid` draws it in `currentColor` so the tab's own colour reaches
    it.

    ## One question decides it, and it is `icon_source`

    There is an icon unless the merchant has switched it off. Not "unless every
    icon setting reads true", which is what this was and why no tab has ever
    shown one.

    Two stored states were being read as "no icon" when neither means that:

    **No value at all.** A Category saved before `icon_source` existed has none,
    and Shopify does not re-default a setting into a block that is already
    saved. So a strict `icon_source == 'icon'` failed on every tab a merchant
    had already added.

    **`icon` stored as the string `none`.** This block's first version put None
    *in the icon list*, before presence became `icon_source`'s job, and a
    merchant who picked it then still has it saved. The option is not offered
    any more, so it cannot mean "no icon" — it means the icon was never really
    chosen, and the default applies. `icon_source: none` is how a merchant says
    they want none now, and it is the only thing that switches one off.

    `at_least: 10` on the size for the same reason: a stored `icon_size` from a
    schema that no longer offers it resolves through `icon-size`'s own fallback,
    and a `--icon-size: 0px` is an icon that is present, correct and invisible —
    the hardest kind to find.
  endcomment
  comment
    ## An icon name this block does not offer draws nothing at all

    This is the reason the pills have been shipping without glyphs, and the
    reason it looked like the icon settings simply did not work.

    `snippets/icon.liquid` is a `case` over names, and its `else` branch renders
    *nothing* — deliberately, so an unknown name never puts a placeholder glyph
    in front of a customer. The cost of that policy is that a wrong name fails
    invisibly: the `<svg>` is still written, still sized, still in the DOM, and
    still has not one path in it. Nothing in the editor or the markup says so.
    A pill with its name and no glyph is exactly what that looks like, which is
    why the failure kept being read as the copy into the strip dropping the
    icon.

    A name gets to be wrong the ordinary way. This block's icon list was a
    bespoke twelve-value select before it was the theme's own, and Shopify keeps
    a stored setting value whether or not the schema still offers it — so a
    Category saved against the old list still holds the old name, the editor
    shows the select with nothing matching it, and `icon.liquid` draws a blank.

    So the stored name is checked against the list this block actually offers,
    and anything else falls back to the default rather than to an empty square.
    The check is a substring test over one string with the separators kept on
    both sides, so `art` cannot match `heart` and `grid` cannot match `grid-2`.

    `icon.liquid` keeps its policy: it is right for a theme-wide snippet that
    cannot know what its callers will pass. The guard belongs at the call site,
    which is the only place that knows which names are real.
  endcomment
  assign pill_icon_allowed = 'star,check,heart,tag,truck,refresh,lock,clock,location,mail,note,info,shield,grid,moon,leaf,droplet,bolt,brain,pulse,sun,sparkle'
  assign pill_icon_haystack = ',' | append: pill_icon_allowed | append: ','
  assign pill_icon_needle = ',' | append: s.icon | append: ','

  assign pill_icon = s.icon
  unless pill_icon_haystack contains pill_icon_needle
    assign pill_icon = 'heart'
  endunless

  assign has_pill_icon = true
  if s.icon_source == 'none'
    assign has_pill_icon = false
  endif

  capture pill_icon_px
    render 'icon-size', size: s.icon_size, custom: s.icon_size_custom
  endcapture
  assign pill_icon_size = pill_icon_px | plus: 0 | at_least: 10

  comment
    ---- the carousel ----

    How many rows are on screen at once. It is the section's setting, not the
    block's: a strip of pills where one category shows three rows and the next
    shows five is a list that changes height every time a pill is pressed, and
    the tray beside it moves with it.

    `columns_*` is what `carousel-options` and `carousel-vars` both call the
    slide count, in either axis — so the row count is passed under that name
    rather than as a second set of parameters meaning the same thing sideways.

    The tablet value is the desktop one. The picker is a column beside the tray
    on a laptop and a full-width block below it on a tablet, and in both cases
    what is being chosen is the height of the list, not its width.
  endcomment
  assign rows = section.settings.rows_per_view | default: 3 | plus: 0
  assign rows_mobile = section.settings.rows_per_view_mobile | default: 2 | plus: 0

  comment
    How many cards the empty state draws: **Products to show**, which is the
    same number that decides how many the filled state draws.

    It was **Rows on screen** — the carousel's height — so the empty category
    previewed as a list with nothing below the fold however many products the
    merchant had asked for. Picking a collection then grew the list from three
    cards to twelve, and the scrollbar, the dots and the arrows all appeared at
    once: the onboarding state was a different list from the one it stood in
    for, and the one number a merchant had actually set was the one it ignored.

    The rows on screen are untouched by this. They are still the section's
    `rows_per_view`, still passed to the carousel as `columns_*` below, and a
    placeholder list is still the same height as a real one — what changes is
    only how far it scrolls, which is exactly what **Products to show** means.

    Real products are already capped at `limit` by the `for` loop's own
    `limit:`, so both states now count the same way and cannot drift.
  endcomment
  assign placeholder_rows = limit | at_least: 1

  assign has_products = false
  if collection != blank and collection.products.size > 0
    assign has_products = true
  endif

  comment
    The slide count is what the list actually holds, and for a real collection
    that is the smaller of what it has and what the merchant asked for — the
    `for` loop below stops at `limit`, so a collection of forty behind a limit
    of six is six slides. It was `collection.products.size`, which handed
    `carousel-options` forty and let it decide a loop was safe on a track with
    six things on it.
  endcomment
  assign slide_count = placeholder_rows
  if has_products
    assign slide_count = collection.products.size | at_most: limit
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The rows, captured, because `carousel-shell` takes its slides as a string.

  That is the shell's normal calling convention and it works here — the rows are
  a `for` loop's output, not theme blocks — so this panel uses the whole
  component rather than the `carousel-shell-attrs` / `carousel-shell-tail` pair,
  which exists only for sections whose slides *are* blocks and therefore cannot
  be captured.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture rows_markup -%}
  {%- if has_products -%}
    {%- for product in collection.products limit: limit -%}
      {% render 'bundle-row',
         product: product,
         index: forloop.index0,
         show_rating: show_rating,
         show_quantity: show_quantity,
         max_per_product: max_per_product,
         id_prefix: panel_id %}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      No collection chosen, or one with nothing in it. The panel draws the
      product tile it will draw when there is one — the same snippet, the same
      markup, the same spacing and typography, with placeholder pictures and
      placeholder copy — rather than a sentence where the list goes.

      The merchant is told what to do in the editor, through this block's own
      `paragraph` setting, which is where an instruction belongs. The storefront
      shows the shape.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- for i in (1..placeholder_rows) -%}
      {% render 'bundle-row',
         placeholder: true,
         index: forloop.index0,
         show_rating: show_rating,
         show_quantity: show_quantity,
         max_per_product: max_per_product,
         id_prefix: panel_id %}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Per-tab colours override the strip's, and the strip's override the scheme's —
  all four, resting and selected, because a strip where one category is the
  brand's colour and the rest are the scheme's is a real thing to want and the
  selected state is half of it.

  They ride on the source element's own `style` attribute and are copied onto
  the button: a cloned fragment carries the classes and styles of the nodes
  inside it, but these four belong on the button itself, which is the one thing
  that cannot travel by being cloned.

  Each is a custom property rather than a declaration, so an unset colour falls
  through to the next level instead of painting `background: ;` over it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture pill_style -%}
  {%- if s.tab_background != blank -%}--bundle-tab-background: {{ s.tab_background }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.tab_color != blank -%}--bundle-tab-color: {{ s.tab_color }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.tab_active_background != blank -%}--bundle-tab-active-background: {{ s.tab_active_background }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.tab_active_color != blank -%}--bundle-tab-active-color: {{ s.tab_active_color }};{%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  ================================================================
  The pill is drawn here, by Liquid, where it stands
  ================================================================

  It used to be published beside the panel as inert markup for
  `assets/collection-tabs.js` to put in the strip, and that is the whole reason
  the icon has never appeared. Every route the module had into the strip ran
  through a copy — cloning child nodes, then re-reading `innerHTML`, then moving
  nodes — and a copy that comes back short has nothing to say so. The label
  survived every time because the module could rebuild that from `data-label` on
  its own; the icon had no second source, so "name, no glyph" is precisely what
  each of those failures looked like.

  So nothing is handed to JavaScript any more. The `<label>` below *is* the
  pill: rendered by Liquid, in the panel's own block, with the theme's `<svg>`
  inside it, present in the HTML the server sends. There is no step between
  rendering it and seeing it, which is the only version of this that cannot lose
  the icon.

  ## Then how does it get into the strip?

  It does not have to. `assets/base.css` lays the pills out as a strip without
  moving them: `.product-bundle__cat` is `display: contents`, so this block's
  two children are laid out by `.product-bundle__panels` rather than by this
  wrapper, and `order` puts every pill on the first line and every panel below.
  The strip is a row in a layout, not an element — which is what it had to
  become, because the element it used to be could only ever be filled by script.

  A block cannot read its children's settings in Liquid: there is no
  `block.blocks`, which is why the Tabs block could not draw these buttons and
  why the module existed at all. A block *can* draw its own, and that is the one
  thing the constraint never ruled out.

  ## The radio is the tab state, and CSS reads it

  `<input type="radio">` with one `name` across the strip, and the panel shown
  by `:has(> :checked)`. No script decides which category is open.

  Native radios also give the keyboard behaviour the ARIA tab pattern asks for —
  one tab stop into the group, arrow keys between the options, the chosen one
  announced as "2 of 4" — which `collection-tabs.js` was hand-rolling as a
  roving tabindex. It is the same interaction with nothing to keep in step, and
  it works before any script loads and if none ever does.

  The input is `visually-hidden` rather than `display: none`, because a hidden
  radio cannot be focused and a strip nobody can tab into is worse than one that
  looks slightly different. The `<label>` is what is seen and what is clicked.

  No `checked` in the markup: a block does not know whether it is the first one,
  and `forloop` does not reach inside `content_for 'blocks'`. So the first
  Category is styled open by CSS until a customer chooses — see the
  `:not(:has(:checked))` rule in `assets/base.css`.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  `block.shopify_attributes` is on the panel below, not on this wrapper.

  This element is `display: contents` — it has no box — and the Theme Editor
  outlines the block it is told about and scrolls to it. A block whose root
  generates no box is a block the editor cannot draw a rectangle around. The
  panel is the half of a Category that is visible when it is open, and it is
  what the editor was effectively highlighting before, so it keeps the
  attributes. `assets/collection-tabs.js` walks up to this wrapper from
  whatever the editor selects.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="product-bundle__cat" data-bundle-category>
  {%- comment -%}
    Not `.visually-hidden`: that utility is `position: static` again on
    `:focus-within`, which is right for a skip link and wrong for this — the
    radio would become a real radio button on the page the moment a keyboard
    reached it. `.product-bundle__pill-input` in `assets/base.css` hides it the
    same way and stays hidden when focused; the focus ring is drawn on the pill
    instead, which is the thing the customer is actually looking at.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <input
    class="product-bundle__pill-input"
    type="radio"
    name="bundle-strip-{{ section.id }}"
    id="{{ tab_id }}"
    aria-controls="{{ panel_id }}"
  >

  {%- comment -%}
    The four `--bundle-tab-*` colours ride on the pill itself, so one category
    can be the brand's colour while the rest are the scheme's. Each is emitted
    only when the merchant set it, so an unset one falls through to the strip's
    and then to the scheme's instead of painting `background: ;` over both.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <label
    class="product-bundle__pill"
    id="{{ tab_id }}-label"
    for="{{ tab_id }}"
    style="{{ pill_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  >
    {%- if has_pill_icon -%}
      <span class="product-bundle__pill-icon">{% render 'icon', icon: pill_icon, size: pill_icon_size %}</span>
    {%- endif -%}
    <span class="product-bundle__pill-label">{{ label }}</span>
  </label>

<div
  class="product-bundle__panel{% unless has_products %} product-bundle__panel--placeholder{% endunless %}"
  id="{{ panel_id }}"
  role="region"
  aria-labelledby="{{ tab_id }}-label"
  data-bundle-panel
  data-block-id="{{ block.id }}"
  data-label="{{ label | escape }}"
  {% render 'spacing', block: s %}
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>

  {%- comment -%}
    `gap_css` rather than `space_between`: the rows sit 48px apart on a desktop
    and 24px apart on a phone, and `data-options` carries one `spaceBetween`
    that Swiper reads once. Handing the property instead puts the breakpoint
    entirely in CSS, where it already lives — `carousel.js` measures the used
    `row-gap` and re-measures it on every resize, so Swiper follows the media
    query rather than predating it.

    `count` is the real number of slides, so `carousel-options` can refuse a
    loop the slide count cannot support rather than letting Swiper turn it off
    itself and leave the merchant with a ticked box that does nothing.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% render 'carousel-shell',
     id: carousel_id,
     children: rows_markup,
     direction: 'vertical',
     detached: true,
     layout: 'carousel',
     layout_mobile: 'carousel',
     tag: 'ul',
     label: label,
     classes: 'product-bundle__carousel',
     track_classes: 'product-bundle__list',
     columns_desktop: rows,
     columns_tablet: rows,
     columns_mobile: rows_mobile,
     gap_css: 'var(--bundle-row-gap)',
     count: slide_count,
     settings: section.settings %}

  {%- comment -%}
    The arrows and the pagination, outside the clip, pointing back at the
    carousel by id — the pattern `carousel-shell`'s own documentation describes
    and `sections/feature-cards.liquid` already uses.

    The branching is `carousel-shell-tail`'s, because `detached` turned that
    file's copy off and this is the same decision being made in the same order:
    the control bar carries its own arrows, so it replaces the bare pair rather
    than joining it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign has_controls = false
    if section.settings.carousel_navigation or section.settings.carousel_pagination != 'none'
      assign has_controls = true
    endif
  -%}

  {%- if has_controls -%}
  <div class="product-bundle__controls">
    {%- if section.settings.carousel_pagination == 'controls' -%}
      {% render 'carousel-controls',
         settings: section.settings,
         carousel: carousel_id,
         align: section.settings.carousel_pagination_align %}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- if section.settings.carousel_navigation -%}
        {% render 'carousel-nav', settings: section.settings, carousel: carousel_id %}
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if section.settings.carousel_pagination != 'none' -%}
        {% render 'carousel-pagination',
           style: section.settings.carousel_pagination,
           align: section.settings.carousel_pagination_align,
           carousel: carousel_id %}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>
</div>

{%- comment -%}
  ---- why the schema says `"tag": null` ----

  This note used to live in the schema itself, under a comment-shaped key made
  of two slashes. Shopify rejects that: a schema is validated against a fixed
  set of attributes, and an unknown one fails the whole upload — `Invalid
  schema: is not a valid attribute` — so the file never reached the theme at
  all. JSON has no comments, and a key invented to act as one is still a key the
  validator does not know. Prose about a schema belongs in Liquid, above it,
  which is where this is.

  Shopify wraps a theme block in a `<div>` unless the schema says otherwise, and
  that wrapper would sit between `.product-bundle__panels` and this block's own
  root — which breaks the strip outright, in three ways at once:

    `display: contents` is on `.product-bundle__cat`. A wrapper in front of it
    has a box of its own, so the pill and the panel would be laid out inside
    that box instead of by the panels container — every Category would be one
    item on one line, and the pills would never collect into a strip.

    `.product-bundle__cat:first-child` is what opens the first Category before
    a customer has chosen. Behind a wrapper, every cat is the first child of
    its own wrapper, so every panel would be open at once.

    `:only-child` is what hides a lone pill, and it would match every Category
    in a strip of any length.

  `blocks/_tab.liquid` and `blocks/_product-card.liquid` declare the same thing
  for the same kind of reason.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.product_bundle_category.name",
  "tag": null,

  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "t:blocks.product_bundle_category.paragraph"
    },
    {
      "type": "collection",
      "id": "collection",
      "label": "t:sections.featured_collection.settings.collection.label"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "label",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_bundle_category.settings.label.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_bundle_category.settings.label.info"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "products_limit",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_bundle_category.settings.products_limit.label",
      "min": 2,
      "max": 24,
      "step": 1,
      "default": 6
    },

    { "type": "header", "content": "t:blocks.subheading.headers.icon" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "icon_source",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "none", "label": "t:shared.none.label" },
        { "value": "icon", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon_source.options__2.label" }
      ],
      "default": "icon"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "icon",
      "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.label",
      "options": [
        { "value": "star", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "check", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "heart", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__3.label" },
        { "value": "tag", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__4.label" },
        { "value": "truck", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__5.label" },
        { "value": "refresh", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__6.label" },
        { "value": "lock", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__7.label" },
        { "value": "clock", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__8.label" },
        { "value": "location", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__9.label" },
        { "value": "mail", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__10.label" },
        { "value": "note", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__11.label" },
        { "value": "info", "label": "t:blocks.subheading.settings.icon.options__12.label" },
        { "value": "shield", "label": "t:blocks.product_bundle_category.settings.icon.options__13.label" },
        { "value": "grid", "label": "t:blocks.product_bundle_category.settings.icon.options__14.label" },
